DEFAULT-DENY, MEASURED RATHER THAN CAUTIOUS. Most members of a typed receiver do
NOT return that type: read_text -> str, stat -> os.stat_result, exists -> bool,
open -> file object, glob/iterdir -> iterators, name/stem/suffix/as_posix -> str.
Propagating by default scores 25.9% precision on adversarial fixtures (7 of 27
added boundaries correct) and mints 16 false taint sinks including 2 database
and 2 network. Across 8 Python repos 1,955 of 2,296 (85.1%) hop>=1 propagations
are provably wrong or unverifiable, and 20 of the 21 provably-wrong ones are a
Path->str transition. The allowlist scores 85.7% on the same fixtures and costs
3 boundaries out of 427 (0.7%). Enumerating the PERMITTING case is also the
standing default-deny rule: a blocker list fails open the moment the stdlib
grows a member.

NO BACKSTOP, which is why the allowlist carries the safety. A minted hint
bypasses gate_named_entry and the ambiguous_names net by design. Control: the 19
catalogued pathlib.Path member names -- 10 of them taint sinks, including
`replace` (also str.replace) and `rename` (also DataFrame.rename) -- match ZERO
times with no module hint, and all become reachable the instant a hint appears.

Compared by exact string, never through _module_matches. That predicate is
permissive by design and accepts an unqualified reference as a component suffix,
so it treats a vendored mylib.pathlib.Path as the real one and mints an fs_write
boundary plus a host_fs taint sink for a third-party class that merely shares a
name. The table is keyed by qualified type, so the lookup is a dict miss.

NOT A NEW CATALOGUE, because the concept already has a home.
FileAnalysis.method_return_types (INV-dihos / WI-kuroj) is the language-neutral
return-type registry Go and Rust already populate from parsed signatures; Python
populates it zero times. A first design proposed a ninth YAML catalogue and was
refuted 3/3 on exactly this point. TYPE_PRESERVING_MEMBERS is the stdlib
complement -- those types ship no source for Pass 1 to parse -- stating the same
fact in the same shape. The `/` operator rides as __truediv__, Python's own name
for it, so an operator needs no new vocabulary and no new axis.

PYTHON-SPECIFIC, MEASURED NOT ASSUMED. Only 5 of 14 io_primitives catalogues key
any primitive on a path/file-TYPED receiver. Go, JS, Swift, ObjC, C, C++, Elixir,
Erlang and Haskell structurally cannot have the gap (join returns a bare string,
or the path is an argument, or the catalogue holds no method-kind entries). Rust
has the shape but 83% of its 69-site gap is base receiver typing (the #246
equivalent, which Rust has not done) and only 6% is derivation. Java already has
base typing; 100% of its residual gap is chain-root receivers and 46 of 89
name-level candidates are String.length / Mockito / Eclipse IResource, so a
name-keyed fix there is net-negative. No second language was manufactured.

DISCLOSED GAPS: .parent/.parents[n] are attribute reads (different AST shape,
smaller population) and are not routed; nor are Path.cwd()/Path.home()
(classmethods on the type, not derivations), interprocedural propagation,
`for p in d.iterdir()` loop targets, or self.attr receivers -- each pinned or
recorded as an exclusion. Kotlin and Swift populations are NOT MEASURED: both
failed their positive control (kotlin emits no unresolved method-call edges at
all; swift puts the receiver variable name in the module slot), so their zeros
are instrument nulls, not absence. The prior ~464-call-site estimate for this
family did not reproduce and is superseded.

